Installation procedureInstall Astro3a.OPA and AstroSM.Lib in any OPA directory and Astro.Pic,Astro3a.Pic in the OPD directory. Use Psion Install and the Icon appears. These files should be found in this archive.			Astro3a.doc	This file(your reading it now)			Astro3a.pic	Icon			Astro.Pic		Splash screen			AstroSM.Lib	Sun and Moon functions library			Astro3a.OPA	The Main programHistoryVersion 0.9	Very limited release. Test or Beta  										(20th October 93)Version 1.0	First Release as Zip file.Version 1.01	Fixed Daylight Saving bug.					Extremes of Latitude calculations improved but still not perfect. (4th November)Notes on Astro3aReverse engineering will break my heart. If you really want the Source write me a letter with a very good reason and a bag full of gold sovereigns.Astro3a has taken many weeks of hard work to come together. It is not crippled as it stands because I cannot tolerate crippled software. I bin it immediately! However it may give you that impression as there is no Planets or Stars option. The bottom line is that these options have not been coded as of 27th October 1993. I have plans to incorporate Star and planet data, Eclipse and Star map features soon, depending on the feedback I receive. To receive these bells and whistles please send a letter where and how you are using Astro3a. If you want to include a donation then Gud on yer! However to receive an update I will need at least a Mac 800k floppy or a 1.44M HD 'Big Blue' type disk, and return postage to where-ever you live. I will not return updates unless those requirements are fulfilled. I am currently unemployed and don't have the resources.	Bug reports are gratefully received. This is a complex program and lots of things are likely to have gone wrong.(Especially for all other Locations other than Scotter! where I can't validate them)	I myself am a very occasional astronomer. I like to know when the Sun's up! And therefore the software may be of little practical use to you. I welcome ideas on how to improve Astro3a.Finally There is no warranty on the accuracy of the data. Rise and set times are based on a sea level horizon. If it is correct for location you are probably standing on Wigan pier! But I have been through many examples in the Astronomical Almanac and they work out pretty close.Esoterica	The Moon rise/set calculation is a formidable one even for your Whizbang Mac Quadra 800 or 486 66 Mhz with built in FPU . Don't be alarmed that it takes your Psion 40 seconds or more to nail it. The algorithm used here involves calling SIN & COS nearly 800 times. Just imagine doing it on your Casio Calculator!Notes to Other programmers: I would like access to the World file to use the ROM based Latitude /Longitude. Any one help?Yours 	Mark Hodsman,			KlingKlang Soft			4 Darbeck Rd,			Scotter,			Gainsborough,			Lincs.,			England			DN21 3SU 			Tel. 0724-762130			October 1993Operation:After the Splash screen, press menu key for options.Display	What' up : Works through all available objects to see what's up for the current location.					Currently only Sun & Moon are available. Press 'Calculate' to see the list of 					planets, their compass position, whether they are on their way up 'rising' or 					passed transit 'setting'. An indication of the Elevation is also displayed.					If there are any more pages to view press 'next' or 'previous'. 'Esc' exits.	Stars:		Not available under version 1.00s	Planets:		Not available under version 1.00s	Sun:			Calculates Sun position and Rise/Set times (if enabled). Uses current system 					time.	Moon:		Calculates Moon position and Rise/Set times (if enabled). Uses current system 					time.Settings:			Quickset:	Displays a dialog. 'Location' gives a list of some major cities. 										Their Latitude/Longitude and Timezone are preset. You have the option of 						adjusting 'Daylight Saving' to Yes/No. Select Object gives a list of 								available objects to calculate. Pressing 'Enter' starts calculating and 'Esc' exits 					the dialog.Uses current system time. 	Set Location:	Enables you to enter a description, latitude and longitude for anywhere on 						Earth. All maximum and minimum values are trapped here. 						Longitude: 	0 - 179 degrees 0-59 minutes						Meridian: 		Whether you are situated East or West of Greenwich. 													(Chicago is West, Tokyo is East, etc)						Latitude:		0-89 degrees 0-59 minutes						Hemisphere:	Whether you are situated North or South of the Equator											(Brisbane is South, Edinburgh is North).						On completing this dialog you are presented with the Local Time and date 						dialog. This defaults to the current System date and time. It is possible to 						enter any date from 1/1/1970 to 31/12/2010. (Use 'Tab' to call up a calendar 						for 1 month or Tab-Tab for 3 months etc). Time can also be set to any valid 						value. Time zone should be set to your locations zone. (Chicago is -6, 							London is 0, Brisbane is +10, etc). You have the option of adjusting 								'Daylight Saving' to Yes/No. Select Object gives a list of available objects to 						calculate. Pressing 'Enter' starts calculating and 'Esc' exits the dialog. NOTE 						If you have previously saved your preferences It is not necessary to retype in 						all of the above data.						After succesful completion of these 2 dialogs, your home location then 							appears in the quick setting box until you use 'Set Location again'Options:	Calc Rise/Set:	Toggle between Yes and No for calculating Rise and set times. Typically 							these would only need to calculated once a day. As It can take 40 seconds 							or more to 'crunch' the rising and setting time of the moon you are 									advised to leave it set to 'No' after the first time around in a session. (The 							values only change on a date rollover).	Running Clock:	Toggles between Current Time or 'System Time/ Psion Time' 										(normally set to your locations Local time) and UTC time. (Universal 								Time Coordinated sometimes known as Greenwich Mean Time).	Twilight Calc:	Select between Civil, Nautical or Astronomical Twilight for calculation 							purposes.Special:	Save Prefs:	Saves your home location description, coordinates, timezone etc to a file 							'Astro3a.set' which should be found in the OPD directory.	Load Prefs:	Loads in your home location data from a previously saved 'Astro3a.set 							file'	About 			Some info about Astro3a & KlingKlang software house.	Exit:				Leave Astro3a	The Display ScreenA set of data is displayed on screen showing:	 Calculated Time 		Local Time:		The calculated Time as entered from 'Set Location' or current time (if 								using Quick settings.		UTC:				Local Time +/- Timezone +/- daylight saving		LST:				Local Sidereal Time. GST adjusted for your longitude.		GST:				Greenwich Sidereal Time. A clock regulated by the stars.(The hour 								angle of the vernal equinox).		Zon(e):				TimeZone setting and Daylight saving setting		Dat(e):				Calculated Date	Positional Data:				RA:					Right Ascension. Equatorial coordinates.  The maximum altitude of an 								object is seen when RA = LST		Dec:					Declination . Equatorial coordinates.		Azimuth:			Horizon coordinates. The compass points. 90 deg = East 0 deg = 									North, etc		Altitude:			Horizon coordinates. Negative values indicates below the horizon. 90 								deg is directly overhead.		(Phase)				Planet or Moon phase as a percentage of full (100%)		(Magnitude)		Planets apparent magnitude (It's brightness) and size	Location:		Name:				Description of your location		Latitude:			Locations Latitude in degrees/minutes and East or West meridian		Longitude:			Locations Longituide in degrees/minutes and North or South 											hemisphere.	Local Rise/Set:				Rise:				Local time of rising and Azimuth		Set:					Local time of setting and Azimuth		(Twilight)			Sun only. Shows Times of Twilight (Astro,Civil,Nautical		(Full Moon)		Moon only. Shows date of Full Moon around the date supplied.		(New Moon)		Moon only. Shows date of New Moon around date supplied.		Running Clock:		Displays the current time as the Psion would display it in other 										applications.